From: Asahi Linux contributors <asahi@lists.linux.dev>
Subject: [PATCH] fairydust: USB-C DisplayPort alt mode (11 commits)
X-Summary: USB-C DisplayPort output (the fairydust work)
X-Who-Needs-It: Only needed if your kernel is built from the release branch. Redundant on the fairydust branch, where it is detected and skipped.

Squashed range asahi-7.1.12-1..fairydust from AsahiLinux/linux, which is
exactly the delta between the release tag and the fairydust branch:
11 commits, 0 behind.

  9b65351d usb: typec: tipd: Track data_status changes for CD321x
  d02d57a6 usb: typec: tipd: HACK: Use drm oob hotplug event
  8b222e1e arm64: dts: apple: t8103: Add dp-altmode dts hacks
  ebbd5c7f HACK: arm64: dts: apple: t8103: Mark ps_atc1_common as always on
  f216a6e7 arm64: dts: apple: t8112: Add dp-altmode dts hacks
  1069f56d HACK: arm64: dts: apple: t8112: Mark ps_atc1_common as always on
  c2bd5218 arm64: dts: apple: t60xx: j[34]1[46]: Add dp-altmode hacks
  681a8a38 HACK: arm64: dts: apple: t60xx: j[34]1[46]: Mark ps_atc1_common as always on
  43f070b6 arm64: dts: apple: t60xx: j[34]75: Add dp-altmode hacks
  d3d85a8e HACK: arm64: dts: apple: t60xx: j[34]75: Mark ps_atc1_common as always on
  b8810ad6 arm64: dts: apple: t6020-j474s: Add dp-altmode hacks

This exists so distributions that build the release branch, such as Asahi
ALARM's linux-asahi, can get USB-C DisplayPort output without switching
their kernel source to a different branch.

The base is the release TAG rather than the release BRANCH. The two are
not interchangeable: the asahi branch trails fairydust by whole stable
releases at times, and a range taken from the branch then carries every
unrelated change in between. When this was regenerated, asahi was on
7.1.9 and fairydust on 7.1.12, and asahi..fairydust was 851 files where
the real delta is 16.

Upstream marks several of these commits HACK, and the fairydust branch is
explicitly experimental. In particular the ps_atc1_common power domain is
forced always-on, which has battery implications on a laptop. Only one
USB-C port drives a display.

Not authored here. Provenance:
https://github.com/AsahiLinux/linux/compare/asahi-7.1.12-1...fairydust
---
